  <info>## MODEL
Human Skill Development Through Task-Specific AI Delegation, Group 10, NetLogo 7.0.4. Developed for this project. The rules implement Section 2 of the report.

## USE
Select Setup, then Go. The four sliders retain their selected values during setup; keep them fixed within a run. Saved baselines are AI capability 70, peer weight 0.04, initial skill centre 50, and task difficulty centre 55. Other parameters are in set-baseline-parameters. One hundred stationary humans occupy a 10 by 10 toroidal grid with eight neighbours each. Blue shading uses a gradual RGB gradient: 0% is visible dark navy, 50% is medium blue, and 100% is white. Everyone starts at 50%. Centred display-only labels show total skill change since setup, with a sign and one decimal place; they update after every round. Label positioning is sized for the saved 10-point view font.

## TASK ROUNDS
Each round gives every human one completed task. Humans first choose delegation using beginning-of-round values, then calculate outcomes, then commit all updates. Task quality is bounded linear capability minus difficulty plus 50. Personal choices maximise expected quality within one percentage point of previous usage. Neighbours receive 4% weight at baseline, except in the first round. Final choices use one-percentage-point steps and retain the adjustment limit. Trust responds to the difference between actual and expected combined quality. Skill changes through independent practice, AI scaffolding, and linear erosion above 50% delegation.

## DISPLAYS
Low, middle, and high groups contain 34, 33, and 33 humans ranked by starting skill, with agent identifier breaking ties. Membership never changes. Cumulative delegation averages completed tasks; recent values average the latest 100 rounds (or all completed rounds when fewer than 100 are available). Recent monitors return false before any task has completed. Skill change compares current and starting skill. The performance plot compares hypothetical quality at 0%, 50%, and 100% AI delegation using current human skill, shared AI capability, and the difficulty slider value. These comparisons do not change actual delegation or skill and are not separate simulated learning trajectories. Scatter plots and recent/performance plots refresh every ten rounds.

## MAIN EXPERIMENT
The main-experiment crosses initial skill centres and AI capabilities from 0 to 100 in steps of 10, and peer weights 0 and 0.04. Difficulty centre is 55. There are 242 conditions, 20 repetitions per condition, and 4840 runs. Each setup selects a new random seed, stores it as run-seed, and both experiments include run-seed in their measurements. Runs last 700 rounds and record endpoint skill change and cumulative delegation for the population and each fixed initial-skill group. Delegation metrics are fractions between 0 and 1. This configuration does not establish that the experiment has been run or that convergence has been demonstrated.

## SUPPLEMENTARY TRAJECTORY EXPERIMENT
skill-delegation-trajectories fixes initial skill centre at 50 and difficulty centre at 55. AI capabilities 20, 50, 70, and 90 are crossed with peer weights 0 and 0.04: eight conditions, 12 repetitions each, 96 runs of 700 rounds. Metrics are recorded at setup and after every round. Skill and latest delegation are recorded for the population and all three fixed initial-skill groups; setup delegation is the 50% starting reference. Both experiments export each group's mean starting skill. These trajectories complement the main experiment's endpoint comparisons; repeated rounds are not independent repetitions.
</info>
